{{also|Scarlet|Scarlett}}
==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*sek-}}<!--if the hypothesis of derivation from Latin sigillatum is correct-->
From {{inh|en|enm|scarlet}}, {{m|enm|scarlat}}, borrowed from {{der|en|fro|escarlate||a type of cloth}}, from {{der|en|ML.|scarlatum|t=scarlet cloth}}, of uncertain origin.<ref>{{R:Etymonline|scarlet}}</ref> This was long thought to derive from {{der|en|fa-cls|سقرلات|tr=saqirlāt||a warm woollen cloth}}, but the Persian word (first attested in the 1290s) is now thought to be from {{der|en|ar|سِقِلَّات}}, denoting very expensive, luxury silks dyed scarlet-red using the exceptionally expensive dye, first attested around the ninth century. The most obvious route for the Arabic word ''siqillāt'' to have entered the Romance languages would be via the Arabic-speaking Iberian region of [[al-Andalus]], particularly [[Almería]], where [[kermes]] was produced extensively; compare especially the dialectal form {{m|ar|سِقِرْلَاط|tr=siqirlāṭ}}. The word then came to be used of woollen cloth dyed with the same dye.<ref>John Munro, “[http://dx.doi.org/10.1163/2213-2139_emdt_COM_550|Scarlet]”, in ''Encyclopaedia of Medieval Dress and Textiles of the British Isles c. 450-1450'', ed. by Gale Owen-Crocker, Elizabeth Coatsworth and Maria Hayward (Leiden: Brill, 2012).</ref> The Arabic word may itself be derived from {{der|en|gkm|σιγιλλᾶτον}}, from {{der|en|la|sigillātus|sigillātum|a type of fabric|lit=sealed; sealing}} {{rfv-etym|en}}.

